From c2c10d4281d7a5ba27df5032c522416dfb35089f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 22:46:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07592/11362] [outer-loop] arc_solver_kit: fix real
 node_frame-aliasing bug found via lf52 investigation

Operator directive: "1 then 2" -- investigate lf52's anomalous
states_expanded=3 first, then complete the live-path question.

Root cause (confirmed via direct diagnostic trace, not assumed):
solve_level() (and its _solve_level_deepcopy/_solve_level_fresh
siblings) captured node_frame = self.last_frame ONCE before iterating
a node's sibling candidates, then passed that SAME reference to
move_pruner.observe() for every sibling. env.step() returns a
distinct Python object each call, but the underlying grid data is a
shared, mutated-in-place buffer -- confirmed by a direct object-
identity test -- so node_frame silently reflected the CURRENT env
state at observe()-call time, not the true pre-action state. Every
genuinely state-changing action therefore looked like a no-op to the
dead-end pruner, which then pruned those same actions as known dead
ends the next time they were tried, closing off lf52's frontier
almost immediately.

Fix: copy.deepcopy(self.last_frame) at each of the three node_frame
capture sites. Verified via a regression test that fails pre-fix and
passes post-fix (confirmed by temporarily reverting and re-running).
Full relevant suite (84 tests) passes clean.

Corrected re-run (largebudget_corrected_20260723.json, corrigendum_of
the buggy largebudget_20260723.json artifact, which is preserved
unedited): lf52 states_expanded 3 -> 140 (now converges cleanly, same
as sc25's unaffected 95). bp35 unaffected, stayed budget-bound at
300/300. levels_gained=0 on all three either way -- the bug affected
exploration depth, not the ultimate null outcome.

Live-path question answered with existing data: results/
arc_live_oracle_gap.json (2026-07-19, the real E3AgentPolicy scored
cascade, frame-only/no-adapter) already shows the same null on
sc25/lf52/bp35 (0/6, 0/10, 0/9) -- a fifth independent mechanism
confirming these are simply hard games for every adapter-free
approach, not a gap specific to being offline-only.

Broader-impact caveat disclosed but not verified: this shared-
infrastructure bug could affect other move_pruner-based measurements
(e.g. HazardMovePruner's tu93 numbers) predating this fix.

